exercises.prompt:
A study in Chile asks whether a twelve-week exercise programme ($T$) lowers blood pressure ($Y$). The analysts believe this causal diagram:

- age affects both joining the programme and blood pressure;
- the programme causes weight loss, and weight loss lowers blood pressure;
- both joining the programme and having low blood pressure make people more likely to attend the final check-up visit.

Which set of variables satisfies the backdoor criterion?

exercises.solution:
**{age}.** The paths between $T$ and $Y$ are:

1. $T \rightarrow Y$ (any direct effect) and $T \rightarrow \text{weight loss} \rightarrow Y$: the causal effect we want, to be left open;
2. $T \leftarrow \text{age} \rightarrow Y$: a back-door path, open unless we condition on age;
3. $T \rightarrow \text{visit} \leftarrow Y$: blocked by the collider, *unless* we condition on the visit.

Adjusting for age blocks path 2 and leaves the others as they should be. Weight loss and the visit are both descendants of $T$, so the criterion excludes them. In practice this means: compare participants and non-participants of the same age, using everyone, including those who missed the final visit (whose blood pressure must then be measured some other way, or the missing outcomes handled with methods for missing data).

